<p>The European Union formally approved a €90 billion, roughly $106 billion, loan package for Ukraine after Hungary lifted its veto, giving Kyiv a much-needed financial bridge for the next two years as it fights Russia’s invasion and tries to keep its economy running. Several reports said economists had warned Ukraine could begin running short of money by June without the disbursement, adding urgency to the decision. The package also signals a shift in European planning toward a longer war. The New York Times reported that this round of aid is more heavily weighted toward military spending than many previous EU assistance packages. At the same meeting, the EU adopted a 20th sanctions package against Russia, underscoring that the bloc paired new financial backing for Ukraine with fresh pressure on Moscow.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/B4B05hYWFJs8TlgqxszoW-wsc5pfSH9sg-KZu5QuK4alLCUa3Qj5GYi6hlnrW5Qs9f8zLvnMvxHJzzJxpJgAP5KS3mC4ZlnAKD7rxCFll1h-US9ugkfJY8DRWeAPm5RhmxrubiRegdTswp3usXxEwCJ3Ax1HkWYHHfixtCxiLdqKvB1H2XguN8JT0xZKS-ucu108OEaI7DDid-dJP37qDrfcYikDWpIR8xlb4vzWHOg-WwospKtNIYKO05U5ls6jOXA5GCaPEFSPkIoVTAyBO9gFFOEfxQxVNfP4bW4mRUd2sYSNeQI9XdDveP9-MVE5OA5lSClNUb-lhda988HIyi8qPTYoMlmA_I7V8G07gk3aZtWdIv3P6zgR1lRalz0ePaOOXXjUw8cCK9yYSpks67IM60SHX3Z_ZHdVH4Xx_aPRBbxSs2AoQvlYy0hRLILgvvNqc732VitbaMvDnvcZ0lbXmsQtci_23NH-4VWDxWSWB490v680xLPAYNBv' alt='Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky comments on the EU’s approval of a €90 billion loan package.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>First tranche: President Volodymyr Zelensky urged the EU to release the first tranche by May or June, a sign of how quickly Kyiv needs the money to cover wartime obligations.</li><li>Military weighting: Unlike several earlier EU support packages, this loan is heavily oriented toward military spending, reflecting Europe’s expectation that a near-term peace settlement remains unlikely.</li><li>Russian revenue: Al Jazeera noted a central complication in Europe’s strategy: Ukraine is receiving a multibillion-euro loan while Russia continues to earn substantial oil revenue.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Volodymyr Zelensky: Zelensky welcomed the loan approval and urged EU partners to move quickly on the first disbursement, saying May or June would be important for Ukraine’s finances. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/23/eu-formally-approves-90bn-euro-ukraine-loan-and-new-sanctions-on-russia'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Ursula von der Leyen: The European Commission president called the agreement good news after Hungary dropped its objections. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/23/eu-approves-ukraine-loan-sanctions-russia'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>European policymakers: EU officials and analysts cited by the New York Times described the package as evidence that Europe is preparing Ukraine for a prolonged fight rather than a quick peace. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/23/world/europe/eu-loan-ukraine.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Hungary: By lifting its veto, Hungary cleared the way for approval of both the Ukraine loan and the EU’s 20th sanctions package against Russia after previously blocking them. (<a href='https://meduza.io/en/feature/2026/04/23/eu-approves-large-loan-to-ukraine-and-20th-sanctions-package-against-russia-after-hungary-drops-opposition'>Meduza</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/european-unions-wartime-loan-vital-lifeline-cash-strapped-132317879'>Why the European Union&#x27;s wartime loan is a vital lifeline for cash-strapped Ukraine</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-zelensky-says-the-eus-90-billion-loan-is-a-strong-decision/'>Zelensky says the EU&#x27;s €90-billion loan is a &#x27;strong&#x27; decision</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/23/world/europe/eu-loan-ukraine.html'>$106 Billion Loan Reflects E.U.’s View That Peace in Ukraine Is Far Awa